WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:06.660
In this lesson we will look at the notion of
the differential of a function,

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:13.360
see how to use the differential for
approximate calculations, and also

3
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.780
study higher-order differentials.

4
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:23.830
The differential of a function y = f(x) at a
point x

5
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.585
is the principal part of its increment,

6
00:00:26.585 --> 00:00:30.400
equal to the product of the derivative of the
function

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.557
and the increment of the independent variable,

8
00:00:35.557 --> 00:00:39.930
or argument; it is written dy or df(x).

9
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:47.170
The differential dy is also called the
first-order differential. In other

10
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:52.870
words, the differential of a function equals
the product of its derivative

11
00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.630
and the differential of the independent
variable.

12
00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:02.532
The main theorems about differentials can be
obtained from

13
00:01:02.532 --> 00:01:07.778
the link between the differential and the
derivative and the

14
00:01:07.778 --> 00:01:12.500
corresponding theorems about derivatives. For
example,

15
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.211
since the derivative of a constant function is
zero,

16
00:01:15.211 --> 00:01:18.340
the differential of a constant is zero too.
The differential

17
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:24.860
of the sum, product and quotient of two
differentiable functions

18
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:31.320
is given by the following formulas. As you can
see, the differential dy

19
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.860
differs from the derivative only by the factor
dx.

20
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:41.650
For example, d(sin x) = cos x dx

21
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:48.850
or d(ln x) = dx/x.

22
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:54.300
Let us explain with an example: for the
function y = x² +

23
00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.750
3x − 2 we find the differential. The
differential of a function equals

24
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.739
the derivative of the function times dx.

25
00:02:03.739 --> 00:02:07.400
We find the derivative of the given function,
and

26
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.180
as a result we get the following differential.

27
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.829
Let us clarify the geometric meaning of the
differential.

28
00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:19.970
Let a function y = f(x) be given in some
region,

29
00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:26.650
and let a point M be given in its domain. If
through the point M

30
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.269
we draw a tangent to the graph of the
function,

31
00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:32.890
let us consider the ordinate of this tangent
at the point x + Δx.

32
00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:39.680
In the picture, the segment AM equals Δx,

33
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:46.220
and AN equals Δx. From the right triangle MAB

34
00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:52.480
we get the value of the tangent of the angle
α: it is the ratio of the opposite

35
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:59.840
leg AB to the adjacent leg Δx. Then

36
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:06.570
AB = tan α · Δx. Since tan α is

37
00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:13.430
the derivative of the function, AB equals the
derivative of the function times Δx.

38
00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:20.410
Comparing this result with the formula, we
find that dy equals

39
00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:27.230
AB. So the differential of a function at a
point x

40
00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:32.041
equals the corresponding increment of the
ordinate of the tangent

41
00:03:32.041 --> 00:03:35.890
drawn to the graph when x receives the
increment Δx.

42
00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:41.310
This is the geometric meaning of the
differential.

43
00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:48.000
Now let us see how to find approximate values
of a function using the differential.

44
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.560
The basic, simplest formula for approximating
the value of a function can be written

45
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.200
as follows: Δy is approximately equal to dy.

46
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.977
or f(x) is approximately f(x₀) + f′(x₀)·Δx.

47
00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:12.500
This formula is used to approximate values of
functions.

48
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:20.202
We need to find an approximate value of the
square

49
00:04:20.202 --> 00:04:26.980
root of fifty. Consider the function y = √x.

50
00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:35.460
We need the value of this function at x = 50.
Let us take x₀ = 49.

51
00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:38.049
We find the value of the derivative,

52
00:04:38.049 --> 00:04:41.860
and then the value of the derivative at the
point x₀.

53
00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:48.740
We substitute the quantities we found into the
formula: the square

54
00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:54.160
root of fifty is approximately seven point
zero seven one.

55
00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:59.845
Differentials can be not only of the first
order

56
00:04:59.845 --> 00:05:03.200
but also of the second, third and so on.

57
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:09.350
Let y = f(x) be a differentiable function,

58
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:14.860
and let the argument x be an independent
variable. Then its first differential

59
00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:19.540
is also a function whose differential can be
found.

60
00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.628
The differential of the differential of a
function

61
00:05:23.628 --> 00:05:26.460
is called its second differential, or

62
00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:33.060
the second-order differential, and is denoted
d²y or d²

63
00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:39.840
f(x). When differentiating, we treat dx as
constant,

64
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:46.830
that is, d²y = f″(x)·dx².

65
00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:52.960
The third-order differential is defined and
found in the same way. In general,

66
00:05:52.960 --> 00:06:00.340
the nth-order differential is the differential
of the differential of order n − 1.

67
00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.762
Let us find the third-order differential of
this

68
00:06:05.762 --> 00:06:08.960
function. First we find the first derivative:

69
00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:15.800
we get the expression 12x² + 20. Now we again

70
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:19.553
take the derivative, this time of the second
order:

71
00:06:19.553 --> 00:06:22.570
we get 24x. Finding the third derivative,

72
00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:24.670
we get that its value is twenty-four.

73
00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:31.570
Now, by the formula for the third-order
differential, d³y

74
00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:43.070
= 24·dx³; this is the answer.

75
00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:51.330
In this lesson we looked at another important
notion of mathematical analysis:

76
00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:57.300
the differential of a function and
higher-order differentials, as well as

77
00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:03.340
the technique of approximate calculation using
differentials.
